        "id": "u1-29-ppc-opportunity-cost-shapes",
        "locale": "en",
        "title": "Increasing and constant opportunity cost",
        "takeaway": "Specialised resources normally create a bowed-out PPC and increasing opportunity cost; identical, perfectly transferable resources create a straight line and constant cost.",
        "definition": "Increasing opportunity cost means that successive additions to one output require ever larger reductions in the other, while constant opportunity cost means that the trade-off ratio does not change.",
        "causalChain": [
          "Resources differ in suitability for the two outputs",
          "Resources best suited to the expanding good move first",
          "Further expansion transfers increasingly unsuitable factors",
          "Each extra unit requires a larger loss of the other output"
        ],
        "realWorldExample": {
          "title": "Land differs in its suitability for alternative uses",
          "sourceUrl": "https://www.fao.org/faostat/en/#data/RL",
          "observedAt": "2026-09-29",
          "explanation": "FAOSTAT land-use data distinguish arable land, pasture, and other uses. Soil, climate, and location differ, so moving progressively more land toward one crop is likely to involve rising opportunity cost."
        },
