    "content": {
      "zh-CN": {
        "id": "u1-34-evidence-refutation",
        "locale": "zh-CN",
        "title": "经验证据与证伪",
        "takeaway": "经验研究把理论预测与观察结果比较；反例会削弱或迫使模型修正，但单一研究很少能终结复杂争论。",
        "definition": "经验证据来自系统观察、数据或实验；证伪是发现可靠证据与理论的可检验预测不一致，从而拒绝或修改该预测。",
        "causalChain": [
          "理论产生明确且可观察的预测",
          "研究设计确定数据和比较方法",
          "证据显示预测是否得到支持",
          "不一致促使检查测量、假设或理论机制"
        ],
        "realWorldExample": {
          "title": "随机评价建立政策反事实",
          "sourceUrl": "https://www.povertyactionlab.org/evaluations",
          "observedAt": "2026-09-29",
          "explanation": "J-PAL 的评价资料展示随机分组如何构造没有政策时的比较结果。观察处理组与对照组差异，可检验特定情境中的因果预测。"
        },
        "misconceptions": [
          "没有拒绝假设不等于证明它永远正确。",
          "与预测不一致时既要考虑理论问题，也要检查数据质量和研究设计。"
        ],
        "examApplication": [
          "评价证据时检查样本、指标、反事实和外部有效性。",
          "使用‘支持、未支持、削弱’等谨慎表述，而非绝对证明。"
        ],
        "summary": [
          "理论必须产生可检验预测。",
          "研究设计决定证据能回答什么。",
          "反驳会推动假设和模型修正。"
        ],
        "explanation": "经济理论要与可观察世界连接，必须产生可能被证据否定的预测。研究者使用统计数据、自然实验、随机试验或历史比较，尝试建立可信反事实。\n\n证据具有不确定性。测量误差、样本差异和外部条件都可能影响结果。重复研究和多种方法得到相近结论，会提高信心；相反证据则要求缩小结论范围或修正机制。"
      },
      "en": {
        "id": "u1-34-evidence-refutation",
        "locale": "en",
        "title": "Empirical evidence and refutation",
        "takeaway": "Empirical work compares theoretical predictions with observation; contrary evidence weakens or forces revision of a model, although one study rarely ends a complex debate.",
        "definition": "Empirical evidence comes from systematic observation, data, or experiment. Refutation occurs when credible evidence conflicts with a testable theoretical prediction, requiring that prediction to be rejected or revised.",
        "causalChain": [
          "A theory generates a clear observable prediction",
          "Research design defines data and comparison",
          "Evidence indicates whether the prediction is supported",
          "Conflict prompts review of measurement, assumptions, or mechanism"
        ],
        "realWorldExample": {
          "title": "Randomised evaluation constructs a policy counterfactual",
          "sourceUrl": "https://www.povertyactionlab.org/evaluations",
          "observedAt": "2026-09-29",
          "explanation": "J-PAL evaluations show how random assignment can create a comparison for what would have happened without a programme. Differences between treatment and control groups test a causal prediction in that setting."
        },
        "misconceptions": [
          "Failing to reject a hypothesis does not prove it is permanently true.",
          "When evidence conflicts, data quality and research design need checking alongside the theory."
        ],
        "examApplication": [
          "Assess a sample, measure, counterfactual, and external validity when judging evidence.",
          "Use calibrated language such as supports, fails to support, or weakens rather than absolute proof."
        ],
        "summary": [
          "Theory must yield testable predictions.",
          "Research design limits what evidence can answer.",
          "Refutation drives revision of assumptions and models."
        ],
        "explanation": "An economic theory connects to the observable world only if it generates predictions that evidence could contradict. Statistical data, natural experiments, randomised trials, and historical comparison all help construct a credible counterfactual.\n\nEvidence remains uncertain. Measurement error, sample differences, and context affect results. Convergence across replications and methods raises confidence, while contrary findings may narrow the domain of a claim or motivate a change in the mechanism."
      }
    },
