      "en": {
        "id": "u1-40-keynesian-revolution",
        "locale": "en",
        "title": "The Keynesian revolution and macroeconomic policy",
        "takeaway": "Keynes argued that deficient aggregate demand can keep an economy below full employment, establishing a framework for fiscal and monetary stabilisation policy.",
        "definition": "Keynesian economics holds that expenditure, expectations, and wage-price rigidity influence short-run output and employment, and that public policy can support aggregate demand when private demand is weak and self-correction is slow.",
        "causalChain": [
          "Pessimistic expectations reduce consumption or investment",
          "Lower aggregate demand causes firms to cut output and jobs",
          "Falling income further reduces expenditure",
          "Countercyclical fiscal or monetary policy seeks to interrupt the contraction"
        ],
        "realWorldExample": {
          "title": "Countercyclical stabilisation in a downturn",
          "sourceUrl": "https://www.imf.org/external/pubs/ft/fandd/basics/keynes.htm",
          "observedAt": "2026-09-29",
          "explanation": "The IMF introduction to Keynesian economics describes how public spending or tax reductions can support demand in a slump and why fiscal space should be rebuilt in stronger periods. The multiplier varies with slack, openness, and policy design."
        },
        "misconceptions": [
          "Keynesian policy does not prescribe larger deficits in every period; it is countercyclical.",
          "Additional spending does not produce a mechanically fixed multiplier because crowding out, imports, and expectations alter the effect."
        ],
        "examApplication": [
          "Explain recession through feedback among aggregate demand, output, employment, and income.",
          "Evaluate stabilisation through economic slack, the multiplier, time lags, debt, and an exit strategy."
        ],
        "summary": [
          "Aggregate demand can remain below full-employment output.",
          "Sticky wages and prices slow self-correction.",
          "Macroeconomic policy can stabilise spending under important conditions."
        ],
        "explanation": "The Great Depression challenged the classical expectation of rapid return to full employment. Keynes emphasised that business investment depends on uncertain expectations and that lower household income reduces consumption, allowing deficient demand to reinforce itself.\n\nThe argument helped establish macroeconomics and stabilisation policy. Government budgets, automatic stabilisers, and central-bank action can moderate a cycle, while timing, financing, inflation risk, and long-run supply still require evaluation."
      }
